BEKW-CODE // WALL PLATFORM ADDENDUM
FREEZERWALL — THERMWEAVE Insulation Shell for Walk-In Coolers/Freezers
Status: Conceptual engineering / development BOM addendum Parent spec: WALL PLATFORM (BOM Series I) — completes FREEZERWALL, previously named-only Canon tie-in: THERMWEAVE (bolt-on Seebeck/TEG waste-heat harvest module), FORGE chassis (SUNFORGE/AMBIENTFORGE structural bay), common Wall Platform interface (0.1/0.2 in the base Wall Platform doc)
1. Function
A wall built to hold a hard, stable cold boundary indefinitely, on the same swappable-panel/service-chase platform as every other FREEZERWALL sibling — and to treat the constant hot-side/cold-side temperature differential across that boundary as a standing THERMWEAVE harvest opportunity rather than pure waste, since a walk-in cooler or freezer runs that differential 24/7 for its entire service life.
Unlike DEWALL (managing humidity) or SAUNAWALL (managing heat), FREEZERWALL's core problem is the opposite of BATHWALL/QUIETWALL's occasional-load cases: this is a continuous, unrelenting thermal gradient, which makes it the single best THERMWEAVE application point identified anywhere in canon so far — better than SOLSHADE's day/night cycling or SOLSHADE:LINE's hot-line/walk-in body-differential cycling, because the gradient here never stops.
2. Layer Architecture
Interior cold-side finish → vapor barrier (cold-side) → structural insulation core → THERMWEAVE TEG layer → vapor barrier (warm-side) → structural frame (FORGE chassis) → exterior warm-side finish
Critical rule, same family as BATHWALL's "the finish is not the waterproofing system": THE INSULATION CORE IS NOT THE VAPOR BARRIER. Two separate vapor barriers, one on each side of the insulation core, prevent moisture migration into the insulation from either direction — condensation inside wall cavities is the single most common failure mode in conventional walk-in cooler/freezer construction, and it's what actually kills insulation performance over time, not the insulation material itself aging.
3. Structural Insulation Core
Candidate materials: closed-cell rigid foam panel (polyurethane/polyisocyanurate class), vacuum-insulated panel (VIP) for high-performance/space-constrained applications, or engineered mineral-fiber board where fire rating outweighs raw R-value
Requirements: dimensionally stable across the full freeze/thaw or cooler-cycle range, closed-cell (no open path for moisture wicking), compressive strength sufficient to carry panel/racking loads without insulation crush over time
Thickness by class: cooler-class targets moderate R-value; freezer-class (well below 0°F) needs substantially more insulation depth — same FORGE-8/FORGE-10/FORGE-12 chassis depth options as the rest of the Wall Platform accommodate this without a different structural system, just a deeper bay
4. THERMWEAVE TEG Layer
Mounted at the insulation core's warm-side face, riding the standing temperature differential across the wall rather than needing its own dedicated hot/cold source — the wall's whole job is already maintaining that gradient
Same ~5-8% harvest efficiency ceiling already established for THERMWEAVE elsewhere in canon — explicitly supplemental, not a real power contributor to running the compressor. The honest framing: a walk-in's refrigeration system is the real energy story here; THERMWEAVE just stops the gradient from being pure waste while it's already there
Because the gradient is continuous rather than cyclical (unlike SOLSHADE's day/night swing or SOLSHADE:LINE's hot-line/walk-in cycling), THERMWEAVE output here should be steadier if lower-peak than those other applications — worth a real comparative efficiency study once any of these get prototyped
TEG wiring routes through the same Channel C harvest-wiring path already standardized in the FORGE chassis, so FREEZERWALL doesn't need a custom electrical routing scheme — it uses the same channel every other FORGE-mounted wall uses
5. Vapor Barriers
Cold-side barrier: placed on the warm-in-winter/room-temperature-facing side relative to the cold interior — i.e., positioned to stop warm ambient moisture from migrating toward the cold core and condensing inside the insulation
Warm-side barrier: stops any moisture on the building's exterior/service-cavity side from migrating inward
Both barriers use continuous, taped/sealed seams — a vapor barrier with gaps is not meaningfully different from no vapor barrier at all
Moisture sensors (optional, same component family as DEWALL/BATHWALL): leak/humidity sensors inside the insulation core cavity itself, catching moisture intrusion before it's visible on either finished face
6. Interior Cold-Side Finish
Washable, impact-resistant, NSF-compatible surface (this wall lives in food-service and cold-storage environments as often as not) — same "replaceable surface over durable core" philosophy as every Wall Platform sibling, since the interior finish is what takes racking impacts and daily cart traffic
Corner and floor-transition details need continuous vapor-barrier integrity, not just a caulk bead — a break at the floor/wall junction is a known chronic failure point in conventional walk-in construction and should get explicit design attention rather than being treated as a finish-carpentry afterthought
7. Service Chase / Door Interface
Standard FREEZERWALL bay carries the usual Wall Platform service chase for electrical (interior lighting, THERMWEAVE bus, optional temperature/humidity sensors) and adds a defined door-frame interface — walk-in door frames are almost always the weakest thermal/vapor point in a conventional build, so FREEZERWALL should treat the door jamb as a first-class designed component, not a site-built afterthought bolted onto standard panel
Door gasket and frame heater strip (standard on commercial freezer doors to prevent frame frost/ice buildup) route through the same service chase
